\section{What the table shows}{

\strong{A single \code{multinom} model.} Predictors as rows, one column group per
non-reference outcome category (spanner = category name). The note
\verb{Reference outcome: <level>.} names the base category (Stata's "base
outcome" line), and \code{outcome_labels} relabels the spanners (e.g.
\code{"Student vs Employed"}). The fit statistics print once, under the
first group. When per-category AMEs are requested, the reference
category appears as a last, AME-only group: its coefficient cells are
empty, since the reference has no equation, and its AMEs complete the
zero sum across categories. The default \code{show_columns} compacts to B,
SE and p as for several models; request atomic tokens to restore the
intervals.

\strong{Several \code{multinom} models, or \code{nested = TRUE}.} One row per
(category, predictor) pair: categories within models would need two
spanner levels. \code{tidy()} and \code{output = "long"} always return the long
form (\code{"<category>: <term>"} rows), whatever the display;
\code{\link[=as_structured]{as_structured()}} and \code{output = "data.frame"} follow the displayed
table (one column set per category), as they do for every other layout.

\strong{An \code{mlogit} model.} Two segments, the presentation of Stata's
\code{asclogit}. The \code{Alternative-invariant} section holds the coefficients
of the alternative attributes, one row each, shared across
alternatives. Then one section per non-reference alternative collects
its intercept and its case-specific coefficients, each against the
reference that the note names (\verb{Reference alternative: <level>.}). The
\code{n} row counts choice situations, not the rows of the long-format data.

\strong{Fit statistics.} See \emph{Fit statistics} below.
}

\section{Values of the shared arguments}{

\describe{
\item{\code{exponentiate}}{Odds ratios of each outcome against the
reference outcome: the baseline-category logits are log-odds
(Agresti 2002; SAS prints "Odds Ratio Estimates" under its
generalized-logit link; Stata's \verb{mlogit, rrr} labels the same
quantity a relative-risk ratio).}
\item{\code{show_columns}}{The AME tokens give one effect per outcome
category for \code{multinom}. They are refused for \code{mlogit}:
marginaleffects computes predictions but not slopes for its
one-row-per-choice data structure.}
\item{\code{outcome_labels}}{For a single \code{multinom} model, the labels of
the category spanners: one per non-reference outcome category,
unique, in model order. The AME-only group of the reference
category, when shown, keeps its own name, and \code{FALSE} changes
nothing, as there is no Outcome row to remove.}
\item{\code{model_labels}}{Refused for a single multinomial model: its
column groups are outcome categories, relabeled by
\code{outcome_labels}.}
\item{\code{vcov}, \code{cluster}}{\code{"classical"} and \code{"CR1"} for both classes.
\code{cluster} has one entry per observation for \code{multinom} and one per
choice situation for \code{mlogit}. See \emph{Standard errors, degrees of
freedom and intervals}.}
\item{\code{nested}}{\code{TRUE} compares nested \code{multinom} fits by
likelihood-ratio test, the convention of \code{anova.multinom()}.}
\item{\code{ci_method}}{\code{"wald"} only.}
\item{\code{standardized}}{Refused.}
