\section{Standard errors, degrees of freedom and intervals}{

The coefficients are tested by Wald z. Cluster-robust \code{"CR1"} comes
from \code{\link[sandwich:vcovCL]{sandwich::vcovCL()}}: the CR0 sandwich scaled by
\eqn{G/(G-1)}{G/(G-1)}, \code{G} the number of clusters. For \code{multinom} it
needs \pkg{sandwich} >= 3.1-2, which added its \code{estfun()} method, and
the AME columns follow it. Under \code{"CR1"}, a nested hierarchy is tested
by the Wald chi-square of the added coefficients on the robust matrix.
}

\section{Fit statistics}{

A \code{multinom} fit defaults to
\code{c("nobs", "pseudo_r2_mcfadden", "aic")}, an \code{mlogit} fit to
\code{c("nobs", "aic")}, its \code{n} counting choice
situations. See \emph{R-squared of glm and categorical models} in
\code{\link[=table_regression]{table_regression()}}.
}

\section{What is refused, and why}{

\itemize{
\item \verb{HC*} for \code{multinom}: a multi-equation model has no working
residuals or hat values, so the HC meat cannot be formed.
\item \verb{HC*} for \code{mlogit}: \code{\link[sandwich:vcovHC]{sandwich::vcovHC()}} computes a result for
it but mis-scales the meat for its per-chooser score structure.
\item \code{"CR0"}, \code{"CR2"}, \code{"CR3"}, \code{"bootstrap"} and \code{"jackknife"}:
\code{"CR1"} is the one robust estimator of these classes.
\item The AME columns for \code{mlogit} (see \code{show_columns} above).
\item \code{model_labels} for a single \code{multinom} model,
\code{ci_method = "profile"} and \code{standardized}.
}
}

